WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.570
Every second, a power grid has to balance supply and demand.

2
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:10.439
When it can't, the control room pays penalties, risks the frequency, or cuts customers off.

3
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:12.825
This is the Demand Response Platform.

4
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:17.340
It turns flexible demand into a grid resource, and runs it end to end.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.353
Deviations from schedule are charged under the DSM regulations.

6
00:00:22.353 --> 00:00:25.646
Renewables swing by hundreds of megawatts in minutes.

7
00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:28.503
Transmission lines congest in specific places.

8
00:00:28.503 --> 00:00:31.733
And the last resort, load shedding, means blackouts.

9
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:39.560
Flexible consumers can solve all of this, if someone can call on them fast, in the right place, and prove what they delivered.

10
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.850
The Overview is a spatial workspace.

11
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:52.160
The live grid fills the screen: substations, transmission corridors, every two-hundred-and-twenty kilovolt station, generation and flexible participants.

12
00:00:52.160 --> 00:01:01.225
The numbers an operator watches float above it: the grid's need, the flexibility available, what is dispatched, and what is actually being delivered.

13
00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:06.484
Here, a demand surge in Bengaluru pushes the state into over-drawal.

14
00:01:06.484 --> 00:01:11.989
The platform detects it, forecasts where it is heading, and opens a demand-response event.

15
00:01:11.989 --> 00:01:22.997
On the right, it answers the four operator questions: what is happening, what is at risk, what happens next, and what the system intends to do, explained from situation to outcome.

16
00:01:24.097 --> 00:01:30.316
Select anything, an alarm, a line, a participant, or the event itself, and the camera flies there.

17
00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:36.471
The affected corridors and participants light up, and a detail card sits right beside the object.

18
00:01:37.571 --> 00:01:45.681
The optimiser picks the cheapest reliable mix of batteries, flexible loads and generation, within network limits, in milliseconds.

19
00:01:45.681 --> 00:01:48.239
A digital twin checks every action first.

20
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:51.858
Inside its autonomy envelope the platform acts on its own.

21
00:01:51.858 --> 00:01:57.348
Larger moves wait for the operator, with dual authorisation above two hundred megawatts.

22
00:01:57.348 --> 00:02:01.528
Then signed commands go out, and delivery responds live on the map.

23
00:02:02.628 --> 00:02:14.297
Every event keeps its own record: target, dispatched and delivered over time, performance against baseline, the participants, the options that were considered, the safety checks, and a live activity log.

24
00:02:15.397 --> 00:02:25.976
Programmes organise the flexibility: interruptible load, industrial curtailment, load shifting, distributed energy and electric vehicles, batteries, and supply-side flex.

25
00:02:25.976 --> 00:02:32.635
Choose one, and its participants light up on the map, with availability, reliability and price at a glance.

26
00:02:33.735 --> 00:02:35.185
Any event can be replayed.

27
00:02:35.185 --> 00:02:41.543
Scrub the timeline, and the map shows exactly who was dispatched, when they acknowledged, and what they delivered.

28
00:02:42.643 --> 00:02:56.211
When an event closes, delivery is measured against each participant's baseline, payments are adjusted for performance, reliability is learned for next time, and every step is written to a tamper-evident, hash-chained audit trail.

29
00:02:57.311 --> 00:02:59.469
And when flexibility is not enough?

30
00:02:59.469 --> 00:03:07.422
The platform proposes rotational load shedding, but only for the over-drawal that flexibility cannot cover, and never on its own.

31
00:03:07.422 --> 00:03:09.641
A shift-in-charge has to approve it.

32
00:03:10.741 --> 00:03:15.376
Non-essential feeders are rostered fairly across the distribution companies.

33
00:03:15.376 --> 00:03:19.157
Spells are limited to forty-five minutes, and two hours a day.

34
00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:24.950
Hospitals, water works, railway traction and defence are never shed, and restoration is staged.

35
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.853
Paid flexibility stays on, so shedding is always released first.

36
00:03:29.953 --> 00:03:33.912
Every two-hundred-and-twenty kilovolt feeder is live on the map.

37
00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:39.788
Shed areas turn red, and each station shows its roster group and the loads that stay protected.

38
00:03:40.888 --> 00:03:48.671
Forecasts carry their uncertainty, predicted violations are flagged ahead of time, and data quality is checked continuously.

39
00:03:48.671 --> 00:03:52.813
When confidence drops, autonomy steps back to advisory on its own.

40
00:03:53.913 --> 00:04:03.663
Autonomy is a policy, not a leap of faith: monitor, advisory, supervised, or autonomous, with a kill switch, role-based access, and full traceability.

41
00:04:04.763 --> 00:04:06.439
Demand Response Platform.

42
00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:11.333
Detect, decide, dispatch and verify, with the operator always in control.

